To display mathematical fonts correctly in a Compose Text Element in Kotlin, you can use the `SpanStyle` class to apply a custom font to specific parts of the text. Here's an example of how you can do this:
```kotlin
import androidx.compose.material.Text
import androidx.compose.ui.text.SpanStyle
import androidx.compose.ui.text.buildAnnotatedString
import androidx.compose.ui.text.font.Font
import androidx.compose.ui.text.font.FontFamily
import androidx.compose.ui.text.withStyle

// Define the font family for the mathematical symbols
val mathFontFamily = FontFamily(Font(R.font.math_font))

// Create the annotated string with the mathematical symbols
val mathText = buildAnnotatedString {
    append("The value of pi is ")
    withStyle(SpanStyle(fontFamily = mathFontFamily)) {
        append("π")
    }
    append(" and the value of e is ")
    withStyle(SpanStyle(fontFamily = mathFontFamily)) {
        append("e")
    }
}

// Display the text in a Compose Text Element
Text(text = mathText)
```

In this example, we first define the font family for the mathematical symbols using the `FontFamily` class and the `Font` class. We then create an annotated string using the `buildAnnotatedString` function, and use the `withStyle` function to apply the custom font to the mathematical symbols. Finally, we display the text in a Compose Text Element using the `Text` function.
Note that you will need to replace `R.font.math_font` with the resource ID of the font file that you want to use for the mathematical symbols. You can add the font file to your project by placing it in the `res/font` directory and then referencing it using the `@font` resource qualifier.
